<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akali movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1920–1925</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Gurdwara Reform Movement, Akalis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Akali movement was a campaign by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> reformers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> from 1920 to 1925 to take control of gurdwaras, Sikh shrines, from hereditary custodians backed by the British. Through mass non-violent protests, or morchas, its volunteers won the Sikh Gurdwaras Act of 1925, which placed the shrines under an elected committee. It gave rise to the Shiromani Akali Dal party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akali-movement.jpg" alt="Akali movement" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jatha_of_Akali_volunteers_marching_to_Guru-ka-Bagh_on_25_Oct_1922.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
